#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 600.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060460} :activity/id #uuid "3a267dc0-721f-5a1c-ad3a-5fa7fec62f8e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048121 :topic/id #uuid "809502b3-ee2f-5a96-8373-13e255b37eac" :topic/math-academy-id 263} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060460 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060872 :tutorial/content "In isosceles right triangles such as the one shown below, the angles always measure $45^\\circ,$ $45^\\circ,$ and $90^\\circ.$ \n\nConsequently, isosceles right triangles are often called **$\\boldsymbol{45^\\circ}$-$\\boldsymbol{45^\\circ}$-$\\boldsymbol{90^\\circ}$ triangles**.\n\n![](images/d3/d32159528abc75f3ed8bcccaedb2765b676fd6cc64164e2f8c81f98eb37f6db4.png)\n\nAll $45^\\circ$-$45^\\circ$-$90^\\circ$ triangles share a special property:\n\n*In any ${45^\\circ}$-${45^\\circ}$-${90^\\circ}$ triangle, the length of the hypotenuse is $\\sqrt{2}$ times the length of a leg.*\n\nTo see why this is, remember that\n\n- in an isosceles triangle, the two legs must have the same length, and\n\n- in a right triangle, the square of the longest side must equal the sum of the squares of the two shorter sides.\n\nSo, if an isosceles right triangle has legs of length $a$ and the hypotenuse has length $h,$ then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{2} & ={a}^{2}+{a}^{2} \\\\ {h}^{2} & =2{a}^{2} \\\\ h & =\\sqrt{2{a}^{2}} \\\\ h & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot a.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "7e5fefd4-4ac4-5999-8c6d-0ad927da9ca6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2381 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "8b5ee65a-7747-5a0e-9d06-721118b74e10" :step/math-academy-id 10416 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060461}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060461 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059630 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059462 :question/id #uuid "d1277863-7d33-566d-91b9-7a78458dca8e" :question/math-academy-id "e-3077"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "73ad4cd2-e7f0-5b7d-82fc-1a8e4b0597d8" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060289} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060290} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497361} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497375}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Length of the Hypotenuse of a 45-45-90 Triangle Given the Legs"} :step/id #uuid "0b3e5ddd-300e-5258-b19c-3499c565bdad" :step/math-academy-id 10419 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060462}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060462 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059631 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059463 :question/id #uuid "ce112319-10a9-5483-82c3-566c0630b23a" :question/math-academy-id "e-3080"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5faa905-dcfc-5eed-b4ed-5818ca6ef342" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048702}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060096} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060282} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497351} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497385} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508040}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Length of the Legs of a 45-45-90 Triangle Given the Hypotenuse"} :step/id #uuid "7049be2a-b0af-5170-a1cb-c440a35c6663" :step/math-academy-id 10420 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060473}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060468 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060873 :tutorial/content "Now that we know about $45^\\circ$-$45^\\circ$-$90^\\circ$ triangles, it's straightforward to see that the diagonal of any square is $\\sqrt{2}$ times the side length of the square.\n\nTo illustrate, consider the square below, where $a$ is the measure of a side and $c$ is the measure of a diagonal.\n\n![](images/07/0743e533eefd4c7e51eaca51c9285fc1b6277553d4f214602a65fc50d1d69ace.png)\n\nSince the sides of a square are congruent, $\\triangle{ABC}$ is an isosceles right triangle (a $45^{\\circ}$-$45^{\\circ}$-$90^{\\circ}$ triangle). \n\nSo, for the diagonal, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c=\\sqrt{2}\\cdot a.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e5c49477-841f-56f9-8779-f1a2a1f115cf" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2382 :tutorial/title "Applications to Squares"} :step/id #uuid "f44a11e0-a932-51d6-97d6-b216a7d42d5f" :step/math-academy-id 10423 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060469}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060469 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059636 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059464 :question/id #uuid "0d244991-c12e-5c56-8f58-762d8be22f8a" :question/math-academy-id "e-3082"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6ae8076e-25bc-5489-ae14-ffa16d17a32c" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060200} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060279} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497395} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497406}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Length of the Diagonal or Side of a Square Given its Perimeter"} :step/id #uuid "247c54c1-1228-55a9-8289-046846fffeeb" :step/math-academy-id 10424} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060473 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059638 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059466 :question/id #uuid "e4466ef9-61d2-5b02-960b-c5c421a9dc1a" :question/math-academy-id "e-3085"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "16fd63e8-664f-53c2-b623-c696b86b5642" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048921}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060221} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060418} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497389} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497415} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508105} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508113} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562467} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575561}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Length of a Hypotenuse or Leg of a 45-45-90 Triangle Given its Perimeter"} :step/id #uuid "718ecaa7-a103-5a7c-a12a-2815eaf19f82" :step/math-academy-id 10430 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060468}}] :activity/title "Isosceles Right Triangles" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059938}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046648} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508162} :activity/id #uuid "d4e27f16-3ada-5048-ad9f-c15bdd8faa37" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048121 :topic/id #uuid "809502b3-ee2f-5a96-8373-13e255b37eac" :topic/math-academy-id 263} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508143 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508113 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32fcd00f-a39a-58b1-925b-6f97038dc7ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507668} {:answer/id #uuid "7a5e9904-c441-5249-95eb-da32c2bf31f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507775} {:answer/id #uuid "b21ebaaf-7316-5810-a179-40920135f9f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507844} {:answer/id #uuid "e5291e91-5e75-563e-9047-4ac169fda707" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507908} {:answer/id #uuid "fa1bd1ce-5d7c-5356-8a41-6149fd10be2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507937}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507937} :answer-field/id #uuid "563ab28a-360a-5519-9504-d8c10d6a3bc9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507979}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ed931f9b-ec89-504b-8f3a-6fc6233992c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-82302" :question/problem "![](images/27/2788f2795065599063c67d9ae7e9e6ee17cae4aa21198450faf7c09ad10765e1.png)\n\nThe perimeter of the above triangle is $6+3\\sqrt{2}.$ Find the measure of each leg." :question/worked-solution "Because the triangle is isosceles, the legs must have the same length. Let $a$ be the length of each leg. So, we have\n\n$$\nAB=BC=a.\n$$\n\nMoreover, because the triangle is a *right* isosceles triangle, the hypotenuse must be equal to $\\sqrt{2}$ times the length of a leg. Therefore,\n\n$$\nAC=AB\\cdot \\sqrt{2}=a\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since the perimeter is $p=6+3\\sqrt{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p & =AB+BC+AC \\\\ 6+3\\sqrt{2} & =a+a+a\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 3(2+\\sqrt{2}) & =2a+a\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 3(2+\\sqrt{2}) & =(2+\\sqrt{2})a \\\\ 3\\cancel{(2+\\sqrt{2})} & =\\cancel{(2+\\sqrt{2})}a \\\\ 3 & =a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the measure of a leg is $3.$"} :step/id #uuid "8730d12c-cd05-5ba6-99b2-4dbdb27838ea" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508149}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508149 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508105 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "241e62f7-500f-5996-9c45-a5607a120603" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507640} {:answer/id #uuid "5288f03e-bcc1-596c-b630-e9cce9ef441f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507722} {:answer/id #uuid "628e1648-d1f4-5cdd-a67a-e29de5ad49a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507743} {:answer/id #uuid "b789fe7c-a826-5709-8e86-6a1815f091c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507851} {:answer/id #uuid "e77d63d4-3768-57eb-87f1-5ab52552457c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507912}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507722} :answer-field/id #uuid "3915ce74-cbdd-56d7-8c28-9b915fda8173" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507962}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d933a602-cca3-50d1-9290-6754d9186974" :question/math-academy-id "q-82303" :question/problem "![](images/70/70d961102240b0c48132baca8db0b77ed80ff8e0d3b7296bd5c727ab2f97dfd3.png)\n\nThe perimeter of the above triangle is $10+5\\sqrt{2}.$ Find the measure of each leg." :question/worked-solution "Because the triangle is isosceles, the legs must have the same length. Let $a$ be the length of each leg. So, we have\n\n$$\nAB=BC=a.\n$$\n\nMoreover, because the triangle is a *right* isosceles triangle, the hypotenuse must be equal to $\\sqrt{2}$ times the length of a leg. Therefore,\n\n$$\nAC=AB\\cdot \\sqrt{2}=a\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since the perimeter is $p=10+5\\sqrt{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p & =AB+BC+AC \\\\ 10+5\\sqrt{2} & =a+a+a\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 5(2+\\sqrt{2}) & =2a+a\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 5(2+\\sqrt{2}) & =(2+\\sqrt{2})a \\\\ 5\\cancel{(2+\\sqrt{2})} & =\\cancel{(2+\\sqrt{2})}a \\\\ 5 & =a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the measure of a leg is $5.$"} :step/id #uuid "a6f7ec19-e013-5d9c-bcfa-d6001e21386c"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508162 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508040 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5f9feb6b-dcf3-574d-a90e-17e5696df9b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507739} {:answer/id #uuid "665ff479-5f6c-53a5-8058-2fe44ac3785d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507749} {:answer/id #uuid "aa4ee9ac-349c-5bdf-9164-c6193cd4e551" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507836} {:answer/id #uuid "bba14005-dce5-5914-a761-712d664bb63a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507857} {:answer/id #uuid "c3eab157-7b55-56d1-9b81-180236a85206" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507871}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507871} :answer-field/id #uuid "71e2b994-15e5-5aeb-82fd-d4bc2277603b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507992}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0589289f-bd62-59fb-8540-3927d969e746" :question/math-academy-id "q-1663" :question/problem "If the hypotenuse of a ${45}^{∘}$-${45}^{∘}$-${90}^{∘}$ triangle has a length of $2$, then what is the length of each leg?" :question/worked-solution "The hypotenuse in a ${45}^{∘}$-${45}^{∘}$-${90}^{∘}$ triangle is equal to $\\sqrt{2}$ times the length of a leg.\n\nHere, we know that the hypotenuse has length $2.$ So, if the length of a leg is $a,$ then we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot a \\\\ a & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the square root from the denominator, we can multiply both the numerator and denominator by $\\sqrt{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{2\\cdot \\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the length of each leg is $\\sqrt{2}.$"} :step/id #uuid "e2059ad8-8e80-5bdc-ac88-748ce810f290" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508143}}] :activity/title "Isosceles Right Triangles" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507606}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125581} :activity/id #uuid "dd6d8fc7-ed4f-5412-9ac7-72ddd6e6fc3d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048164 :topic/id #uuid "84c65e66-54ce-5cc4-9970-19a6aba25468" :topic/math-academy-id 1888} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125475 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124564 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124372 :question/id #uuid "c844db9d-a556-5ce4-90e8-2db1b5671579" :question/math-academy-id "e-6684"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7ec3d139-c43a-5547-b0ae-147e41a55180" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125027} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125099} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233399} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233408} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507528} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522755}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Global Minimum of a Function"} :step/id #uuid "4f190feb-818e-5e2a-8ed1-9a841eb19c11" :step/math-academy-id 24350 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125583}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125581 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125713 :tutorial/content "When we deal with functions, we are often interested in their largest or smallest values.\n\n- The **global maximum** of a function is the largest $y$-value that the function attains on its domain. There can only be (at most) one global maximum. It's also called the **absolute maximum.**\n\n- The **global minimum** of a function is the smallest $y$-value that the function attains on its domain. There can only be (at most) one global minimum. It's also called the **absolute minimum.**\n\nIf one or both exist, the global maximum and minimum values of a function are collectively called the **global extrema** of the function. \n\nFor example, in the graph below, the global maximum is $2$, while the global minimum is $-2$.\n\n![](images/22/2235cefbb995edcc085f96d8a5ff4e1ddd83f6345091c90ec3e0a7797fb9d8f3.png)\n\nThe point where a function reaches its global maximum/minimum is called the **global maximum/minimum point**.  So in this case, \n\n- $(-2,2)$ is the global maximum point, and\n\n- $(1,-2)$ is the global minimum point.\n\n**Watch out!** The *global maximum/minimum* is unique. It's a value of the function ($y$-coordinate on the graph). However, there can be several *global maximum/minimum points*. All of these will have the same $y$-coordinate (*global maximum/minimum*) but different $x$-coordinates." :tutorial/id #uuid "cfedbc54-8dd6-532f-9075-109e336b43a6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1495 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "a61d8ec6-2820-5303-a832-f51a73657cc2" :step/math-academy-id 3625 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125582}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125582 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124624 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124206 :question/id #uuid "30557b46-f1f0-51dc-94ed-5d84515bc4b0" :question/math-academy-id "e-1112"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "86517de9-0e7c-5c00-a3e8-7aeccbf12d9b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048652}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125072} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125176} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507446}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Global Maximum of a Function"} :step/id #uuid "ce76583f-0ff9-522f-8324-5c6a6492916a" :step/math-academy-id 3626 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125475}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125583 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124625 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124207 :question/id #uuid "c7c94a49-1a4e-51f6-b0e7-213c67f40fd9" :question/math-academy-id "e-1113"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d9f1c88f-1c8a-52f9-a144-b52f67564f5e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049072}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125032} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125100}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether the Global Extrema of a Function Exist"} :step/id #uuid "a936946d-a2c6-5c5b-8e3e-ad8903a06ff2" :step/math-academy-id 3629}] :activity/title "Global Extrema of Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124693}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153196} :activity/id #uuid "a9e96cf9-9aad-5b83-81b1-b9488d698494" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048993 :topic/id #uuid "d1b21eb7-a767-5b1f-8e65-566b74a26f39" :topic/math-academy-id 2552} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152982 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152110 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151920 :question/id #uuid "42e81da3-7ea4-5b58-9163-3057df064c21" :question/math-academy-id "e-4460"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "32650c78-38d5-5530-991d-bf0b8f2198cb" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049405} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050948}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152654} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152655} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498546} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498595} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575532}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Graphs of Proportional Relationships"} :step/id #uuid "3e1f0c98-c1f6-59bc-8387-51338e8475ab" :step/math-academy-id 16271 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152983}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152983 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152111 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151921 :question/id #uuid "06bdefa1-8b40-5777-9ca0-9f257834833d" :question/math-academy-id "e-4461"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d9c2ec6a-e04a-5e97-a91c-3a232e9aa29d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050195} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050249}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152644} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152645} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498491} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498526} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520499} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572719}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Constant of Proportionality or Unit Rate"} :step/id #uuid "65b0ae62-9c2f-5d32-a1a1-ac39767edaa8" :step/math-academy-id 16272 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153199}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153196 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153338 :tutorial/content "How does a proportional relationship look when we plot it in the $(x,y)$ plane?\n\nTo answer this, let's consider the following proportional relationship:\n\n$$\ny = 2x\n$$\n\nTo plot a graph, we first need a table of values. \n\n| $x$ | $y$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $0$ |  |\n| $1$ |  |\n| $2$ |  |\n| $3$ |  |\n\nFilling in the $y$-values using the equation $y = 2x,$ we get the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $2$ |\n| $2$ | $4$ |\n| $3$ | $6$ |\n\nSo, we have the following $(x,y)$ coordinates:\n\n$$\n(0,0), \\quad (1,2),  \\quad (2,4),  \\quad (3,6)\n$$\n\nIf we plot these points in the coordinate plane, we get the following:\n\n![](images/a8/a8cd951c66c67b8f28b791011c7553675b3112029d9ebe4b19769127b5e75bd5.png)\n\nThis leads us to the following important fact:\n\n*The graph of any proportional relationship is a straight line that passes through the origin $(0,0)$.*" :tutorial/id #uuid "becbb429-3b12-59c4-8172-aef250c47df9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1507 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "1aec00f2-ca4a-5296-be84-e7e83d0f585d" :step/math-academy-id 3709 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152982}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153199 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153339 :tutorial/content "When there is a proportional relationship between $x$ and $y,$ we can express this using the **proportionality symbol** $\\propto,$ as follows:\n\n$$\ny \\propto x\n$$\n\nNote that the following statements are all equivalent:\n\n- $y = kx$\n\n- $y$ **varies directly** with $x$\n\n- $y$ is **directly proportional** to $x$\n\n- $y \\propto x$" :tutorial/id #uuid "503f0382-0b82-583d-898a-bc1dd1aa02d1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1508 :tutorial/title "Alternative Notations and Terminology"} :step/id #uuid "4dc61433-6c56-58c8-9a95-4614aa5f1bf9" :step/math-academy-id 3712 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153200}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153200 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152241 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151799 :question/id #uuid "1a5e8fef-3361-5d24-9abd-d0474a2cd3ae" :question/math-academy-id "e-1141"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5462223e-a206-572a-846a-378621922011" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050613}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152609} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152868} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498511} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498547} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520477} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572691} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572723}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Direct Variation Equation from a Graph"} :step/id #uuid "e8203c67-93c8-55d2-b5fd-7a7003ecaf79" :step/math-academy-id 3715}] :activity/title "Understanding Proportional Relationships Using Graphs" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186152310}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520589} :activity/id #uuid "00211bb8-0554-55a4-bda1-bdbf208e3686" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048993 :topic/id #uuid "d1b21eb7-a767-5b1f-8e65-566b74a26f39" :topic/math-academy-id 2552} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520541 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520499 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a00323d-f93f-5304-838e-9a2020861786" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\frac{5}{2}$ centimeters per month" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520051} {:answer/id #uuid "2f593f76-c5a7-50c8-9b17-9fcace1dabaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\frac{6}{5}$ centimeters per month" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520063} {:answer/id #uuid "3da9c120-60eb-589b-a9aa-5cbfc90e5257" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$3$ centimeters per month" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520078} {:answer/id #uuid "9340f66f-9d6d-54b6-808d-a0bf629f8943" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\frac{7}{3}$ centimeters per month" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520202} {:answer/id #uuid "b27a859b-f70f-5a32-ba4f-97460ef30f9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\frac{2}{5}$ centimeters per month" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520239}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520051} :answer-field/id #uuid "0752f685-6167-5321-9403-2b36429faec1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520369}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d5876a38-e0d7-5724-8ef9-3dbbcd7f0e46" :question/math-academy-id "q-163005" :question/problem "![](images/4d/4df58d2f35cc69e8a567a65bc64f24e5da8820e1be5d229979b08fe2c3d1cd8f.png)\n\nThe graph above shows a proportional relationship between the time $x$ (in months) since Mary's baby was born and the number $y$ of centimeters he has grown. What is the baby's unit rate of growth?" :question/worked-solution "The baby's unit rate of growth in centimeters per month is simply the constant of proportionality for the given proportional relationship.\n\nTo compute the constant of proportionality, we first need to choose a point $(x,y)$ on the graph. Here, let's choose the point $(2,5).$\n\n![](images/5b/5bbc6c5b4188c5db894ceebe7d347c901afb428d5d102864a90218ef53420a60.png)\n\nNow, we can find the constant of proportionality by computing the ratio of the $y$-coordinate to the $x$-coordinate, as follows:\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{y}{x}=\\frac{5}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Mary's baby's unit rate of growth is $\\frac{5}{2}$ centimeters per month."} :step/id #uuid "3d3d364f-3ab5-5714-9335-f8bd97fe1c65"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520548 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520477 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2dd7620a-2e0a-5048-84e8-580582cc4050" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{3}{2}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520060} {:answer/id #uuid "5c1e780f-72c4-55fe-86bf-f63cbb6742d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{4}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520134} {:answer/id #uuid "67143172-5c75-57e2-9507-5548168537b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520148} {:answer/id #uuid "8052a59d-160b-523d-93b3-2be518aa22c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520175} {:answer/id #uuid "faaf0326-bcd6-5fdb-9b82-fb8c891b09c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520359}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520175} :answer-field/id #uuid "df70821b-247b-5776-8c45-aa8d80611751" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520434}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "817c5709-21ea-5c46-9e06-c595f5917c73" :question/math-academy-id "q-68204" :question/problem "![](images/1a/1a5313995e9740a61b455a9c368aca6223a80c8bbea659f06ec2a63c8260747b.png)\n\nKaren decides to walk from her workplace to her house. There is a proportional relationship between the distance $x$ (in kilometers) she covers and the time $y$ (in hours) it takes her to cover that distance.\n\nWhat is the equation for the relationship between $x$ and $y$ shown in the graph above?" :question/worked-solution "Every proportional relationship takes the form $y=kx,$ where $k$ is the constant of proportionality.\n\nTo compute the constant of proportionality, we first need to choose a point $(x,y)$ on the graph. Here, let's choose the point $(3,1).$\n\n![](images/be/bec01f9dc01b340075f09dd459571fe8199116f18a6ab912eb958c7a6f9ee4f2.png)\n\nNow, we can find the constant of proportionality by computing the ratio of the $y$-coordinate to the $x$-coordinate, as follows:\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{y}{x}=\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{3}x.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "4bbe34c0-1b18-5560-8226-a31de4f5850c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520541}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520589 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152609 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4acc6262-ca31-5c5f-ac0d-1ae4e682b14c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520105} {:answer/id #uuid "a9466f4c-419a-5b13-82db-f0fde5671554" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520223} {:answer/id #uuid "cf4e5346-6676-5c14-868d-660ee8405b0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520283} {:answer/id #uuid "e5402f53-c6f1-5c01-821d-49754b58179c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=8x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520325} {:answer/id #uuid "f98ff76b-d478-5a9c-a544-9213463eb301" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520356}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520356} :answer-field/id #uuid "8bcc8a79-2736-57a1-bfc2-56ec9e252477" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150161}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "878ffbea-7b4e-53ce-97b4-dbbab3af9a6a" :question/math-academy-id "q-51829" :question/problem "![](images/81/815e7943ff4f01bf9a89cd6bb1214cf9266c8ae528cfd53e05a7ac56cf1e0e74.png)\n\nThe graph above shows a direct variation between the variables $y$ and $x.$ Which equation describes the relationship between the variables?" :question/worked-solution "Since $y$ varies directly with $x,$ we use the equation\n\n$$\ny=kx.\n$$\n\nNext, we notice there is a point on the graph with $y=8$ and $x=2.$ So, we substitute this data into the equation to find $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8 & =k\\cdot 2 \\\\ \\frac{8}{2} & =k \\\\ 4 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the direct variation equation is given by\n\n$$\ny=4x.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "96b62d68-1935-5cf6-bfed-287d64594d22" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520548}}] :activity/title "Understanding Proportional Relationships Using Graphs" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519986}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572774} :activity/id #uuid "f24b3b44-c729-5176-9a67-9bc09288acd6" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048993 :topic/id #uuid "d1b21eb7-a767-5b1f-8e65-566b74a26f39" :topic/math-academy-id 2552} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572749 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572691 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a10968a-1243-571b-a9bd-8c6631c847bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "u=-\\frac{1}{2}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572345} {:answer/id #uuid "440df762-731d-56df-bc2f-7e0b6dfe87dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "u=\\frac{1}{5}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572398} {:answer/id #uuid "81b9413e-71d2-55bc-b120-48cf7418e25a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "u=-5z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572455} {:answer/id #uuid "bbcec67a-21f7-515b-b67b-d4dfa9b9a0fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "u=5z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572525} {:answer/id #uuid "c2fd7900-c51f-5bed-9b31-6871651fb51e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "u=-\\frac{1}{5}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572535}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572535} :answer-field/id #uuid "35a7e094-9492-556b-b055-0b4284ba502d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572608}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "528c6dbc-6dbe-5499-88b0-b6881a756455" :question/math-academy-id "q-115639" :question/problem "![](images/9e/9e3a9bc6a2c642697cfa2b6525e3abf42a0227f0ac66eecade156a842fb5e92d.png)\n\nThe graph above shows a direct variation between the variables $u$ and $z.$ Which equation describes the relationship between the variables?" :question/worked-solution "Since $u$ varies directly with $z,$ we use the equation\n\n$$\nu=kz.\n$$\n\nNext, we notice there is a point on the graph with $z=-10$ and $u=2.$ So, we substitute this data into the equation to find $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =k\\cdot (-10) \\\\ -\\frac{2}{10} & =k \\\\ -\\frac{1}{5} & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the direct variation equation is given by\n\n$$\nu=-\\frac{1}{5}z.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "35310a5a-f14e-5220-a85f-b4ffe8767295"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572750 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572719 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d37e1bb-efc8-5170-a04c-6bd70009b86b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$2\\,\\frac{1}{3}$ dollars per pound" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572350} {:answer/id #uuid "2f1efd6d-9c98-5e26-81d8-cffc494aafcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$1\\,\\frac{2}{3}$ dollars per pound" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572372} {:answer/id #uuid "3b7818b8-beef-5b29-af60-15faef7c0a86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$1\\,\\frac{1}{3}$ dollars per pound" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572387} {:answer/id #uuid "4b0a3f95-e65f-5423-819f-da1d3c12223c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\frac{2}{3}$ dollars per pound" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572407} {:answer/id #uuid "9d0d083c-0799-5884-af03-185f6861a496" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$2$ dollars per pound" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572485}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572387} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c911119-b189-59c9-afe1-208ee09fedb8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572615}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "d9eeaf2b-c92c-529e-9218-24a01a5754ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-68193" :question/problem "![](images/25/2501f7e346abf6c79a211fd090162be685c001bc6acc68787d8c73c0eee7a8af.png)\n\nThe graph above shows a proportional relationship between the weight $x$ (in pounds) of ice cream sold by a store and the profit $y$ (in dollars).  What is the constant of proportionality?" :question/worked-solution "To compute the constant of proportionality, we first need to choose a point $(x,y)$ on the graph. Here, let's choose the point $(3,4).$\n\n![](images/7b/7beacc5836ed16a4dcb36916d0273513b7b2d94871c4ee5994d56479bc11f18d.png)\n\nNow, we can find the constant of proportionality by computing the ratio of the $y$-coordinate to the $x$-coordinate, as follows:\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{y}{x}=\\frac{4}{3}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{3}=1\\,\\text{R}1=1\\,\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the constant of proportionality is $1\\,\\frac{1}{3}$ dollars per pound."} :step/id #uuid "36a5066a-12cf-5071-9d56-323a5a484789" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572749}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572774 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572723 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1746c645-2ca1-5886-a22d-da171cd15a01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "L=50t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572342} {:answer/id #uuid "4e190a43-d3de-5ac0-b00b-dc513b13624d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "L=60t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572411} {:answer/id #uuid "56acae82-639f-5413-88a9-a7b04e1a0938" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "L=90t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572418} {:answer/id #uuid "b3983d19-ed17-57aa-997b-ec527346494e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "L=30t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572517} {:answer/id #uuid "ea1c18ae-83f8-5cdc-a1af-f2cbf0066c61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "L=15t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572570}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572570} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf78e6b5-1138-5983-a5e7-632caf3ecb6f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572642}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "eabcc9c8-1fae-5b8f-9d65-4f29f12ab5ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-51883" :question/problem "![](images/fa/fa895816cc537fc2ccb30f99f2f9c8ec01cf0ee75ba76fc8a2aed1c0b3349206.png)\n\nThe graph above shows that the volume of gas $L$ in a truck's tank is directly proportional to the time $t$ since the driver started filling it. Find a direct variation equation that describes the relationship between $L$ and $t.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $L$ varies directly with $t,$ we use the equation\n\n$$\nL=kt.\n$$\n\nNext, we notice that there is a point on the graph with $t=6$ and $L=90.$ So, we substitute this data into the equation to find $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}90 & =k\\cdot 6 \\\\ \\frac{90}{6} & =k \\\\ 15 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the direct variation equation is given by\n\n$$\nL=15t.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "7d66a73b-7d01-5490-b561-23c518b44d9a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572750}}] :activity/title "Understanding Proportional Relationships Using Graphs" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572316}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 900.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204741} :activity/id #uuid "534c3fc9-4831-5fca-ad59-09b388796c6f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049199 :topic/id #uuid "e2d5739b-5d8f-5b44-8b7e-0b4e50252e45" :topic/math-academy-id 4951} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204741 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205291 :tutorial/content "When factoring a trinomial, we should always begin by factoring out any constant factors. Then, we factor whatever quadratic remains using the techniques we've studied. \n\nFor example, let's factor the following trinomial:\n\n$$\n6y^2+21y+9\n$$\n\nThe first thing to notice is that all the terms have a common factor of $3.$ So, we can simplify the calculation by first factoring out $3\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n3(2y^2 + 7y + 3)\n$$\n\nSo, our goal now is to factor the trinomial in parentheses:\n\n$$\n2y^2 + 7y + 3\n$$\n\nTo do this, we find two numbers that \n\n- multiply to the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant ($2 \\times 3 = 6$), and\n\n- add to the linear coefficient ($7$).\n\nAlso, since all coefficients are positive, we only need to consider the *positive* factor pairs of $6.$\n\nThe positive factor pairs of $6$ are\n\n$$\n(1,6),\\quad (2,3).\n$$\n\nNotice the numbers in the factor pair $(1,6)$ add to $7{:}$ \n\n$$\n1+6 =7  \\quad{\\color{green}\\checkmark}\n$$\n\nThen, we write the linear term as $y+6y,$ and factor the result by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{y}^{2}+7y+3= \\\\ 2{y}^{2}+y+6y+3 & = \\\\ (2{y}^{2}+y)+(6y+3) & = \\\\ y(2y+1)+3(2y+1) & = \\\\ (y+3)(2y+1) & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original trinomial factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{y}^{2}+21y+9 & = \\\\ 3(2{y}^{2}+7y+3) & = \\\\ 3(y+3)(2y+1) & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we're done!\n\nWhenever the coefficient of the quadratic term is negative, we should always factor this out first. That way, we're left with the task of factoring a trinomial containing a positive quadratic coefficient. Let's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "35b6c679-c9c6-5b44-8de5-21106bc70674" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9456 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "97ca27d9-5627-500f-b4ce-94d3d62acc77" :step/math-academy-id 178290 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204742}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204742 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203942 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203744 :question/id #uuid "f57cd9ee-7613-5083-948a-2434078d6796" :question/math-academy-id "e-15071"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "caf03377-f06d-5831-80b8-dcbf4978a8db" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048375} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049023}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204183} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204336} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508114}] :knowledge-point/title "Quadratics With Positive Coefficients (After Initial Factoring)"} :step/id #uuid "53a8db76-c802-563a-80d3-ee07f4085259" :step/math-academy-id 178291 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204743}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204743 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203943 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203745 :question/id #uuid "c6a7913e-d53e-5885-b30c-87148e86032d" :question/math-academy-id "e-15088"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b49cf980-1078-593b-bf56-c9a132c41273" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204338} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204345} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508054} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508073} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575616}] :knowledge-point/title "Quadratics With Negative Constants (After Initial Factoring)"} :step/id #uuid "408a8b32-052b-5f8b-9893-3e4c9e92fd6e" :step/math-academy-id 178295 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204744}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204744 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203944 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203746 :question/id #uuid "81afbe63-1358-5d90-999d-4797f5f44f78" :question/math-academy-id "e-15089"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e1a8957-9964-5eba-b5f4-b0e1dd525838" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204184} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204337} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508106}] :knowledge-point/title "Quadratics With Negative Linear Coefficients and Positive Constants (After Initial Factoring)"} :step/id #uuid "73b63fbf-6b48-571e-9ab0-6b574e48cf7a" :step/math-academy-id 178299}] :activity/title "Further Factoring Trinomials With Leading Coefficients" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186204138}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055618} :activity/id #uuid "a163662d-b871-5e67-90b7-3c408d42da30" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049330 :topic/id #uuid "ec62124a-ba1a-5287-92fd-a64e499120c1" :topic/math-academy-id 101} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055618 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055982 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to solve the following system of equations:\n![](images/01/01eee66dd39797b90503716f097ff604a4f339d8ea0a25ceef9b8ea7a0898ab4.svg)\n\nTo solve systems of equations *graphically*, we first draw each of the equations in the same coordinate system. Then, the solution of the system is given by the common points where the lines intersect.\n\nLet's see how this method works for the system above. To plot the functions given by the equations, we first rewrite them by making ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg) the subject in each equation.\n![](images/c7/c7778dd2c82032ef68ad8221a990abe1b2bafb4e36a7a0e2640a43856be58c5a.svg) \n\nThen, we draw the lines in the same coordinate system:\n\n![](images/47/47fb02cf8bb4668b24034302fd4265ec5261f4d244cd9d9d599afeffd91d2424.png)\n\nThe lines intersect at the point ![](images/d8/d87c68d6226f4bb9ecf52ab576a5ac45c83fbb02c83edd1af04ac235dd518467.svg), which is the solution to our system.\n\n![](images/b3/b3b95135ad9a758f891280b15e8bd6f6165bf9f47a76a1c99280af02dad78911.png)\n\nWe can also use the graphical method to solve systems of equations where the corresponding functions are not straight lines. These types of systems are called **nonlinear systems**. Let's consider such an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "e7d63cd6-92ea-5bb3-adc8-f9d09bf2d24d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3407 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "8d314c2a-668c-507d-a140-8f47f8422fff" :step/math-academy-id 19145 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055619}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055619 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054664 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054561 :question/id #uuid "e8d3ec4c-5ab2-5bd1-9e8f-32d3526a7ca7" :question/math-academy-id "e-5204"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2812e564-d5a3-58f6-a679-f8d3d20c042e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048678} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048838}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054991} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054992} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508924} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508938} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509000} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537980} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575582}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving Systems of Equations Containing Quadratic Functions"} :step/id #uuid "487c6fc8-1141-5f82-85ea-4e560f81130f" :step/math-academy-id 19148 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055620}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055620 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054665 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054562 :question/id #uuid "fced2a80-0cad-5405-b0bb-c12e4a7cce06" :question/math-academy-id "e-5205"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd4dde78-84d1-5b33-a41a-9f10b24131b6" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055258} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055259} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508941} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508942} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537893}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving System of Equations Graphically"} :step/id #uuid "74edc604-916f-54b2-899e-637369c1a92d" :step/math-academy-id 19149}] :activity/title "Solving Systems of Nonlinear Equations Using Graphs" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054864}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538021} :activity/id #uuid "18cfbf29-0714-53c4-bf61-75276fdf84b2" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049330 :topic/id #uuid "ec62124a-ba1a-5287-92fd-a64e499120c1" :topic/math-academy-id 101} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538002 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537893 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01a5189d-540f-5143-b180-eea39c520ccb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-1,1)$ and $(2,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537228} {:answer/id #uuid "1b84224c-2aef-5acf-9174-2e514405a489" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-1,0)$ and $(0,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537285} {:answer/id #uuid "1d898613-60f0-545a-a43b-ab2df0a31df5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(1,-1)$ and $(1,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537292} {:answer/id #uuid "7cc2dfcf-d574-5c58-9c7c-5fd06dbeeee6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(0,2)$ and $(1,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537473} {:answer/id #uuid "aab67e8e-c1fd-5b7a-a619-a599bce9e1a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-2,0)$ and $(-1,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537552}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537228} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c0ebced-cf33-5e38-93fa-adaeaf32a0f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537816}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1ecf42ce-06ef-5bfd-b762-67d3f1697496" :question/math-academy-id "q-40946" :question/problem "![](images/e1/e1892e632e2485b17400ba9d2a9372bf164e20812c3b6fe4f9b03ab1348fab7d.png)\n\nUsing the graphical method, find the solutions $(x,y)$ of the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=f(x) \\\\ y=g(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The solutions $(x,y)$ of the system correspond to the intersection points of the two curves.\n\n![](images/8d/8dfab4aa640e596227bd6a6a09af36264f6bce9bc0906a69ea433d6e6a3db78d.png)\n\nFrom the graph, the curves intersect at \n\n$\\,$ $(-1,1)\\,$ and $\\,(2,1).$ \n\nTherefore, these are the solutions of the corresponding system."} :step/id #uuid "2d2a8d38-4a66-5619-979f-ca1ea501fd0f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538061}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538021 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054992 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0144b8df-ea7b-5d97-a90d-b6a729498469" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(0,1)$ and $(-1,-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537226} {:answer/id #uuid "0d132648-29c9-5efd-8dd9-74bc9443ede2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(0,1)$ and $(1,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537249} {:answer/id #uuid "2d56859a-7919-5fa1-b36a-db3eb7b258c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-1,0)$ and $(2,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537324} {:answer/id #uuid "9de3af1d-692f-5053-99f2-3783795f8e29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-1,4)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537523} {:answer/id #uuid "dabcb025-40e7-5f2c-ae63-f8732b21b6f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(1,0)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537666}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537249} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f34e030-e9d1-56f0-806f-3268b6a34932" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051482}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8c76a9c4-fcd2-5048-9771-c38b93693e80" :question/math-academy-id "q-12329" :question/problem "![](images/ae/aee38b4fa46a403ee9517fc5a087434c8205b6553a4ebefa43be74ba7babfbc0.png)\n\nUsing the graphical method, find the solutions $(x,y)$ of the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=x+1 \\\\ y={x}^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The solutions $(x,y)$ of the system correspond to the intersection points of the two curves.\n\n![](images/56/563850386ee40e291a04911a2216614bda567ffbb8cfce1a7713400900d2701c.png)\n\nFrom the graph, the curves intersect at \n\n$\\,$ $(0,1)\\,$ and $\\,(1,2).$ \n\nTherefore, these are the solutions of the corresponding system."} :step/id #uuid "504ad451-e9ae-5979-b879-13ec6623b2ca" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538002}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538061 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537980 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f5bbd1a-b6f0-537f-a093-4b3d1be271cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(2,1)$ and $(-1,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537393} {:answer/id #uuid "57e6a003-f73c-515c-a06f-ba516c1963bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-2,-3)$ and $(1,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537409} {:answer/id #uuid "6d485cb2-89c5-5022-b5b4-533fc087f5b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-2,3)$ and $(1,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537441} {:answer/id #uuid "8cc80659-76af-5a9d-b845-d8d0123dc768" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(1,3)$ and $(1,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537496} {:answer/id #uuid "b7fdda5c-b3a2-5a24-9521-9f6e08ac1fa7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-2,-1)$ and $(-1,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537586}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537441} :answer-field/id #uuid "f66e49fb-db34-54b1-8488-6385f866a2fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537875}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e75342f3-2efc-5d3d-a3c3-d44c19646c04" :question/math-academy-id "q-78292" :question/problem "![](images/e6/e6831eff5d8feb87ebba8e7d449d98fbe72c3ed4df01d8374d4866e8e3456ddc.png)\n\nUsing the graphical method, find the solutions $(x,y)$ of the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=-x+1 \\\\ y={x}^{2}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The solutions $(x,y)$ of the system correspond to the intersection points of the two curves.\n\n![](images/e1/e1ebbb0ce147dc986dd4d47539f055407c9b870af2ada9629520c4fa1d9aef33.png)\n\nFrom the graph, the curves intersect at \n\n$\\,$ $(-2,3)\\,$ and $\\,(1,0).$ \n\nTherefore, these are the solutions of the corresponding system."} :step/id #uuid "e6565653-a052-5dc4-ab06-3fb284893e9e"}] :activity/title "Solving Systems of Nonlinear Equations Using Graphs" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537207}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106853} :activity/id #uuid "21a47620-e3ce-58d1-b236-202e42fddc8e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049388 :topic/id #uuid "f0cae3f1-329e-50d8-bc2d-f933c5aca17d" :topic/math-academy-id 1397} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106853 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107053 :tutorial/content "The **area of a triangle** can be computed using the formula\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = \\dfrac{b \\cdot h}{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $b$ is a *base* of the triangle and $h$ is the corresponding *height*.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's compute the area of the triangle with base \n$b = 4$\nand the corresponding height is\n$h = 8,$\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/84/84de11aac8e9f33a459f2a83647dac43eb2054445d842ac769511711366a15ec.png)\n\nThe area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{b\\cdot h}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cdot 8}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{2} \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** At the end of the lesson, we'll demonstrate where the formula comes from. But first, let's get some practice using it." :tutorial/id #uuid "bf61df49-32c4-50ee-a6f4-21f9da391a02" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2306 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "4293d690-2bd3-5e64-91c6-7e02a0c764f9" :step/math-academy-id 416085 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106854}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106854 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105870 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105541 :question/id #uuid "fc5fc31e-f585-5d15-8a8a-eb0d19704e9a" :question/math-academy-id "e-2947"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b6c58537-344e-5842-b68c-d9b5a9d7ca96" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048472} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049608}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106402} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106403} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253331} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284686} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332895} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392749} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508965} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508973}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Area of a Triangle"} :step/id #uuid "a5cb1ab1-345e-5828-babf-f43dc62b43f0" :step/math-academy-id 416086 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106855}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106855 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105871 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105542 :question/id #uuid "cdb1d145-6e8a-5213-a7f7-8076440cbbeb" :question/math-academy-id "e-2949"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6445834c-7788-5427-a72c-30b06cc8e6ab" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050428}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106193} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106194} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354593} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354594} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354604} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354605} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399961} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549052} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573258}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Area of an Isosceles Right Triangle"} :step/id #uuid "bf4e1141-e78b-57d6-8b32-737373cca813" :step/math-academy-id 416089 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106856}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106856 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105872 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105543 :question/id #uuid "58e1ce31-f6fc-5900-ab89-e00241045b12" :question/math-academy-id "e-2950"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a7656d23-06c9-5d22-b62a-3636bca66ab6" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106155} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106388} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253335} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253338} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386869} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404426} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508991} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508996} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549027}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Base or the Height of a Triangle Given Its Area"} :step/id #uuid "e81bf9a1-61c3-5dc2-8537-01fb6d1a7871" :step/math-academy-id 416092 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106857}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106857 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107055 :tutorial/content "We know that the area formula for a triangle is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = \\dfrac{b \\cdot h}{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $b$ is a base and $h$ is the corresponding height.\n\nBut where does this formula come from?\n\nTo understand where the formula comes from, consider $\\triangle{ABC}$ below.\n\n![](images/02/02c1ace5f18e9204c1559f08f591ecd692f00691c48cbee81bc4426c3b34400e.png)\n\nLet's draw a rectangle around $\\triangle{ABC},$ as follows:\n\n![](images/89/893026324410235de9cb7ac286867006593a451556587c60219fe4375a4218a4.png)\n\nWe have two pairs of right triangles with equal areas (each pair is indicated with arrows).\n\nSo, the area of $\\triangle{ABC}$ is exactly half of the area of the rectangle. This gives\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A}_{ABC} = \\dfrac{AB \\cdot CH}{2}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b5be18d9-4833-50c8-8800-085d64e21ce8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2311 :tutorial/title "Proof of the Formula"} :step/id #uuid "d47b1a6f-6091-5f5b-832a-9ce0b04d1526" :step/math-academy-id 416095}] :activity/title "Areas of Triangles" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186106016}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549099} :activity/id #uuid "58c010c8-8685-5554-94e6-4a528432d38e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049388 :topic/id #uuid "f0cae3f1-329e-50d8-bc2d-f933c5aca17d" :topic/math-academy-id 1397} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549084 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549052 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "417abf8e-a35f-5695-b467-ef4d6b12c3f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548479} {:answer/id #uuid "5cfd8bf4-6acb-5b60-99c2-c71861b1536a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548526} {:answer/id #uuid "a449dec1-300a-5c71-8723-ab8f3583b39a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548678} {:answer/id #uuid "b32a6fda-8006-5e0b-bc5e-adba5d208d71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548714} {:answer/id #uuid "e8a23dd6-a819-573e-923b-b6ed5c0e58fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "32.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548810}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548479} :answer-field/id #uuid "20a135df-b545-5cd9-8ae5-be9b531bfe60" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548881}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d45bf6d6-3e38-52ed-a276-f99fe5dd268f" :question/math-academy-id "q-87851" :question/problem "![](images/67/678c719915b0d27c1378548267123cfbac3f13f13ee0a05fd6dacd85164911f6.png)\n\nWhat is the area of the right triangle $ABC$ shown above?" :question/worked-solution "Since $m∠A=m∠C={45}^{∘}$ and $AB=BC,$ we have an isosceles right triangle. \n\n![](images/4d/4d340b37161cb3f970ba588fa15a21b3a35ee376c5766e92d6c48358d4ed003e.png)\n\nTherefore, the area of the triangle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}AB\\cdot BC \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {5}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 25 \\\\  & =12.5\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "1d366aad-cd67-5343-baa4-8059cf6699a9" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549111}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549099 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549027 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ed3ec42-7887-59f4-8607-49a2c72ca3d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "49\\,cm" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548449} {:answer/id #uuid "498f62c4-d10e-5980-af7b-cf193748dc55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "18\\,cm" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548494} {:answer/id #uuid "68718dec-ecd8-5ae5-a90a-23ccd4586c90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10\\,cm" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548552} {:answer/id #uuid "897c0883-8ea7-5679-b872-375f1438d23b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7\\,cm" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548619} {:answer/id #uuid "b854d192-b8ff-551c-8ab1-aef38e4dfe2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "14\\,cm" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548723}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548619} :answer-field/id #uuid "8da7771e-63f1-5e10-be3c-1af25ccedf2e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548930}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8d086598-d0f7-52ac-bba9-a6017b092d64" :question/math-academy-id "q-21051" :question/problem "![](images/c8/c85e6ee974a59d956367634fdd1d57b65e9d42a980fe1750886527b173f05d32.png)\n\nFind $BM$ given that the area of $△ACB$ is $49\\,{cm}^{2},$ $AM=5\\,cm,$ and $MC=9\\,cm.$\n\n(Figure not drawn to scale)" :question/worked-solution "The area of the triangle is defined as \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{b\\cdot h}{2},\n$$\n\n where $b$ is the base and $h$ is the corresponding height.\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the height is $h=BM,$ and the corresponding base is \n\n$$\nb=AM+MC=14\\,cm.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the formula for the area, we can solve for the height as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{b\\cdot h}{2} \\\\ 49 & =\\frac{14\\cdot h}{2} \\\\ 98 & =14\\cdot h \\\\ h & =\\frac{98}{14} \\\\ h & =7\\,cm\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, $BM=7\\,\\text{cm}.$"} :step/id #uuid "51c06ce5-a234-5a1a-aa5e-9042f0827d88" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549084}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549111 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354604 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2db54839-6a5b-5b75-9ac0-2fe3487ce8c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "800" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351989} {:answer/id #uuid "43e30190-4e81-5fda-b3a5-079b052411c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352191} {:answer/id #uuid "43f1421d-10d0-5af6-8262-60e55e5615e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352192} {:answer/id #uuid "50c4006c-d6f4-5cb3-977c-4236359a9cc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "200" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352294} {:answer/id #uuid "7d349513-c9f4-5a55-81fa-6b46f8d0946e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "400" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352666}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186352294} :answer-field/id #uuid "23513061-aa39-51dd-a874-2807ff166615" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353872}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2e4dded3-cef3-55b9-ba8c-e50ddf3e7619" :question/math-academy-id "q-87853" :question/problem "![](images/16/1616f8d921cbdaf1a5654354292e261b0941e5dbfcee16ae620c8a1cf3e92135.png)\n\nWhat is the area of the right triangle $ABC$ shown above?" :question/worked-solution "Since $m∠A=m∠C={45}^{∘}$ and $AB=BC,$ we have an isosceles right  triangle. \n\n![](images/3f/3f9a52834fe76f1560beec8580dbd91076a9f7c6ef980f410aa5bfe7da162c9c.png)\n\nTherefore, the area of the triangle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}AB\\cdot BC \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {20}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 400 \\\\  & =200.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "7462aa22-72f5-51d7-bde0-cc38a35c0d8c"}] :activity/title "Areas of Triangles" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548343}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084061} :activity/id #uuid "da88a97d-6c4e-5299-b921-71200664bd9d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049474 :topic/id #uuid "f80da52a-4a96-5269-8885-c600a798d61e" :topic/math-academy-id 790} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084061 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084526 :tutorial/content "Let's plot the graph of ![](images/e3/e31f27c483c52d7933201e37d1f6020c20d7903402be16f28ffd407c7f3db93a.svg). According to the algebraic definition of absolute value, we have\n![](images/eb/ebc6cc8f3964043ffa2ff15f7e3501222794753f30577e4b71037efb2e4ffa30.svg)\nSo, the left-hand branch of the graph is the function ![](images/8f/8f96aa73ce843c7815a1fed6bf3f3774a173cfa4d56f13e1f3a8c48d53cd7097.svg), and the right-hand branch is ![](images/23/238e977be23f692486c8bf68f6b40255435b3d1796cc50d260a014a8c670be7b.svg):\n\n![](images/60/60ad4dfdb6086f20f5137c5b4ac4905e55d5697ec4cf31575bf79be5fd408e0c.png)\n\nNotice that the graph has a sharp corner at ![](images/c1/c1e8c20213b45135f50bdc8d4544220f7b0f943ff3ca49439660c86d017e789f.svg) We call this point the **vertex** or the **minimum point.**\n\nWe can apply **horizontal** and **vertical translations** to the graph of ![](images/e3/e31f27c483c52d7933201e37d1f6020c20d7903402be16f28ffd407c7f3db93a.svg).\n\n- The graph of ![](images/91/91c9b5c48d46f81336e889febab90a9027a96accef8626bd0743ca81042f25b8.svg) is a **vertical translation** of the absolute value graph ![](images/0c/0cb4d1ae0c2e2451edd095b9dbcca94222e8ef0514db46745a2acce4b617d78e.svg) It moves the graph ![](images/34/349015631ec44ef653936c85586d2c5ef56ba64392b3f92b4b92cbef3e731bc5.svg) units up.\n\n![](images/cd/cd8b826034f7c3668cacb490e0fab60daaf7c5cae63975bfa5cae9d468c7dec6.png)\n\n- The graph of ![](images/30/3016548be8be38ace3450d51a10371543820a3357b1adbf809d69c23c70f14bb.svg) is a **horizontal translation** of the absolute value graph ![](images/0c/0cb4d1ae0c2e2451edd095b9dbcca94222e8ef0514db46745a2acce4b617d78e.svg) It moves the graph ![](images/34/349015631ec44ef653936c85586d2c5ef56ba64392b3f92b4b92cbef3e731bc5.svg) units to the *right*.\n\n![](images/65/65912d2831dd525964b78a20f2a707d7d828daa2a5e2ebd0793c6b337a42e360.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- When drawing horizontal and vertical translations, it is easiest to transform the graph's vertex first. Then, the two branches can be drawn coming out of the vertex.\n\n- When we translate the graph ![](images/87/87f4b5227cb69bd468d75d826fe448c91ae677181f9142fcbe9bc757d0acea77.svg) the resulting curve is called the **image** of ![](images/e3/e31f27c483c52d7933201e37d1f6020c20d7903402be16f28ffd407c7f3db93a.svg) under the translation." :tutorial/id #uuid "1b8dce6a-8742-581b-80f6-e39c9943b395" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9295 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "bdb8f706-56c3-508a-a745-c4cf4b268e01" :step/math-academy-id 169443 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084062}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084062 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083189 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082947 :question/id #uuid "5a2db9be-6ef3-5466-a7cc-4e7e2b5670ec" :question/math-academy-id "e-14919"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "61ec00a6-06ae-5d78-bb1a-b06351432cc3" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049534} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049844}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083554} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083623} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506600} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572189}] :knowledge-point/title "Graphing a Vertical Translation"} :step/id #uuid "6d0878f5-845c-5163-b1ae-e72f37806b6d" :step/math-academy-id 169444 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084063}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084063 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083190 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082948 :question/id #uuid "2e688f8d-16cc-58d6-8791-538cc335ce04" :question/math-academy-id "e-14925"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "05cffafa-cfcf-5979-a413-b7d0b66dd434" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083624} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083676} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506528} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570741} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570773}] :knowledge-point/title "Graphing a Horizontal Translation"} :step/id #uuid "dfd03723-b5ce-5ca8-98bb-2b1cf56700d6" :step/math-academy-id 169447 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084064}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084064 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084528 :tutorial/content "Horizontal and vertical translations can be combined. It does not matter which we do first.\n\nIn general, the graph of $y=|x-h|+k$ is a translation of the graph $y=|x|$ by\n\n- $h$ units right, and\n\n- $k$ units up." :tutorial/id #uuid "b55739df-8b0d-5ef2-9702-d56de0246597" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9302 :tutorial/title "Combining Translations"} :step/id #uuid "3287422a-9367-5f91-9957-0152a7e772df" :step/math-academy-id 169450 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084065}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084065 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083191 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082949 :question/id #uuid "82de263c-391b-5d45-bbd6-353c31c806fc" :question/math-academy-id "e-14933"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca5dcf12-db1a-5cfc-b369-986caa8f666c" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083546} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083625} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083628} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506530} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506595} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570706} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570714} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570739} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575563}] :knowledge-point/title "Combining Horizontal and Vertical Translations"} :step/id #uuid "09be9587-52a8-510c-841a-fe31ae2eb30e" :step/math-academy-id 169451}] :activity/title "Absolute Value Graphs" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186083468}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570848} :activity/id #uuid "b4da87d0-f5a5-50ee-a9f0-c6cb2fb5fc32" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049474 :topic/id #uuid "f80da52a-4a96-5269-8885-c600a798d61e" :topic/math-academy-id 790} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570841 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570706 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "189651b0-54f3-5292-acb1-78996d20c50d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570173} {:answer/id #uuid "80b350c2-d6af-593a-8af1-2ec69b5c8cad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570352} {:answer/id #uuid "933e034e-403a-57ae-b3fe-587b30e14b03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570383} {:answer/id #uuid "aa9212d0-88ad-5f2e-997d-59a47e64e7c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570425} {:answer/id #uuid "dc88590b-2d68-5698-b5ee-ad190de36855" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570511}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570425} :answer-field/id #uuid "7196b41d-0d52-543a-bb1d-8738e6064d5e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570631}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "17bccb94-8027-5ec1-b42e-ebe5775a61ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-15814" :question/problem "Find the minimum value of $y$ on the graph $y=|x-3|+3.$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that $y=|x-h|+k$ is a vertical translation of the absolute value graph $y=|x|$ by $h$ units right and $k$ units up.\n\nThe function $y=|x-3|+3$ has $h=3$ and $k=3.$ Therefore, it is a translation of the absolute value graph $y=|x|$ by $3$ units *right* and $3$ units *up*.\n\n![](images/d5/d5c67a156d80c6dabd832539a4912c2bdd7ef38af9735fcade7ee46e4ba66eaa.png)\n\nLooking at the graph, we can see that the minimum value of $y$ is $3.$ This occurs at the point $(3,3).$"} :step/id #uuid "878cfbc6-5471-58e8-915b-20a46b57e956" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570844}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570843 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570773 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23d06b4f-bc10-59f4-bad3-9be438d8e2e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(0,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570189} {:answer/id #uuid "3ba1fb6c-8327-51bf-8fb0-05e23abf8c83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-1,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570222} {:answer/id #uuid "4cf6e7fe-b201-5c9d-a8a4-bd5f53457d4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(1,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570259} {:answer/id #uuid "a4336403-9070-5be9-84ed-ebf6940c61a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(0,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570415} {:answer/id #uuid "e950df02-b773-5199-bf04-fc99be23ac4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(1,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570532}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570222} :answer-field/id #uuid "a0a5060b-5648-5c20-ac0c-e7491147f963" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570651}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "bc3a1a0d-0a47-5924-a45a-6ae72b37f737" :question/math-academy-id "q-78063" :question/problem "If $y=|x+1|$, what are the coordinates of the minimum point on the graph?" :question/worked-solution "Remember that $y=|x-a|$ is a horizontal translation of the absolute value graph $y=|x|$ by $a$ units right.\n\nThe function $y=|x+1|$ has $a=-1.$ Therefore, it is a horizontal translation of the absolute value graph $y=|x|$ by $1$ unit left.\n\n![](images/38/38532ccdd10c7a60f26fdf35c837376af799d7b024370e8b223cd61b7b18eb9d.png)\n\nLooking at the graph, we can see that the coordinates of the minimum point are $(-1,0).$"} :step/id #uuid "9272e140-eade-50e0-aa74-a463e9e7aca4" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570857}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570844 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570741 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d75859b-74a6-5c31-b94d-cfb3fec63748" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-5,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570202} {:answer/id #uuid "53d0ffba-e11b-5c18-a494-dc8db0e90b44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(0,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570272} {:answer/id #uuid "7df37ea3-f56a-521e-9c32-559774529837" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(0,-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570346} {:answer/id #uuid "96e0d1b5-5c60-5283-a61c-715d74c22923" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(0,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570388} {:answer/id #uuid "ca1bc01f-1907-59c5-ae47-53cddb450003" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(5,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570475}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570475} :answer-field/id #uuid "b93eba92-6655-5d26-b15a-6487f0a03dd6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570667}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7432d94a-3593-5858-b61c-052181b6a281" :question/math-academy-id "q-15826" :question/problem "If $y=|x-5|$, what are the coordinates of the minimum point on the graph?" :question/worked-solution "Remember that $y=|x-a|$ is a horizontal translation of the absolute value graph $y=|x|$ by $a$ units right.\n\nThe function $y=|x-5|$ has $a=5.$ Therefore, it is a horizontal translation of the absolute value graph $y=|x|$ by $5$ units right.\n\n![](images/ca/ca70d305bb0da32441611d879ca2a4a2453b38e951b448964b5d48f01fa33e28.png)\n\nLooking at the graph, we can see that the coordinates of the minimum point are $(5,0).$"} :step/id #uuid "9437c58f-f80b-51f5-a33f-6de7c5ebdc3a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570843}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570848 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570739 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04656707-2a2c-5ffa-bbcb-9540a7b1d0c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570130} {:answer/id #uuid "091b549b-9482-5c9d-99a3-414abc46226c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570138} {:answer/id #uuid "96ce8727-2f4f-5e50-b3b2-f6937e57145d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570387} {:answer/id #uuid "9cff91ed-11f3-5a4c-b5a3-c02c22d2774b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570402} {:answer/id #uuid "e8201c4f-d73c-5a75-aee0-069d79e730fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570529}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570138} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f1dab20-875a-5ecd-83ee-e9779ffd70fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570604}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6dff94b8-5d8c-56b5-9363-d9107e29b4d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-15811" :question/problem "Find the minimum value of $y$ on the graph $y=|x+4|-1.$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that $y=|x-h|+k$ is a vertical translation of the absolute value graph $y=|x|$ by $h$ units right and $k$ units up.\n\nThe function $y=|x+4|-1$ has $h=-4$ and $k=-1.$ Therefore, it is a translation of the absolute value graph $y=|x|$ by $4$ units *left* and $1$ unit *down*.\n\n![](images/45/45411bac1f347093b8b9931e1f8f8bee4f1eb411b6256a4c56d0ba33cffe603e.png)\n\nLooking at the graph, we can see that the minimum value of $y$ is $-1.$ This occurs at the point $(-4,-1).$"} :step/id #uuid "a2af62b9-442c-506b-9cce-b6a9e9270c22" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570841}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570857 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570714 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ee5d6999-dacf-585b-9763-55851b2e5a28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570541}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570541} :answer-field/id #uuid "aecd1abd-2502-5a3e-aece-0d12a409100b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570660}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2b65f25b-10a0-5e3f-8752-11a285fdf596" :question/math-academy-id "q-222360" :question/problem "The minimum value of the function $y=|x+3|+2$ is $y=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Remember that $y=|x-h|+k$ is a vertical translation of the absolute value graph $y=|x|$ by $h$ units right and $k$ units up.\n\nThe function $y=|x+3|+2$ has $h=-3$ and $k=2.$ Therefore, it is a translation of the absolute value graph $y=|x|$ by $3$ units *left* and $2$ units *up*.\n\n![](images/81/81bace946b2fb425efa7fd7c54eb53ec353be5d9ef1516d9cf24e2c13656a60b.png)\n\nLooking at the graph, we can see that the minimum value of $y$ is $2.$"} :step/id #uuid "c6cb8059-d840-5375-a89b-c4dc968b16b1"}] :activity/title "Absolute Value Graphs" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570110}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 660.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065162} :activity/id #uuid "0c7c191d-53bd-5ae5-94c5-4eed4a0e0009" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049500 :topic/id #uuid "f967432c-0e8a-553d-8497-053cde92d843" :topic/math-academy-id 361} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065162 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065375 :tutorial/content "To multiply a binomial and a trinomial, we take each term of the binomial and multiply it by every term in the trinomial. Then, we simplify by collecting like terms.\n\nFor example, to multiply $({\\color{blue}{x}} + {\\color{red}{3}})$ and $(x^2 + 2x+4),$ we multiply ${\\color{blue}{x}}$ and $(x^2 + 2x+4),$ and then we add this to the product of ${\\color{red}{3}}$ and $(x^2 + 2x+4).$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)({x}^{2}+2x+4) & = \\\\ x({x}^{2}+2x+4)+3({x}^{2}+2x+4) & = \\\\ {x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}+4x+3{x}^{2}+6x+12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we group the like terms and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}+4x+3{x}^{2}+6x+12 & = \\\\ {x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}+3{x}^{2}+4x+6x+12 & = \\\\ {x}^{3}+5{x}^{2}+10x+12\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "abdf5593-94a7-5f4c-a2ec-ca378d5b0034" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4089 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "1805ec56-b520-54eb-bdc6-f80e213e4e8e" :step/math-academy-id 24547 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065163}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065163 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064276 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064119 :question/id #uuid "5aa1e9b2-a6d3-507d-8530-9cbba2fc2162" :question/math-academy-id "e-6707"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "43f97f24-06a1-50cc-9c73-48bc491c632f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050818}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064957} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064958} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233791} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234404} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483405} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547040}] :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying a Binomial and a Quadratic Trinomial"} :step/id #uuid "3ba36e49-21df-5a7b-a5eb-2a39da5e23fa" :step/math-academy-id 24548 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065164}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065164 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064277 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064120 :question/id #uuid "55f28c00-cfe7-507e-830f-f7d993d16687" :question/math-academy-id "e-6710"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f7019d52-33a1-5968-9a93-2f2f06fbb0d6" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064677} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064959} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233141} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234405} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483417} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483427} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493900} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493963}] :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying Trinomials"} :step/id #uuid "28e7da43-48d0-58cf-b2d4-a4737212e95c" :step/math-academy-id 24563 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065165}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065165 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064278 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064121 :question/id #uuid "9e77dab4-4e8b-51df-a070-1a42fcdbf212" :question/math-academy-id "e-6711"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "09357892-5d4b-5346-99b0-9babc7b0d574" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064736} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064737} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233706} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233790} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483375} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483394}] :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying Binomials and Trinomials Containing More Than One Variable"} :step/id #uuid "09d4e779-1be2-5451-af22-a6a22455d666" :step/math-academy-id 24564}] :activity/title "Multiplying Polynomials" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186064448}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046648} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493976} :activity/id #uuid "7d34f865-13dc-53b8-9562-0158bac35795" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049500 :topic/id #uuid "f967432c-0e8a-553d-8497-053cde92d843" :topic/math-academy-id 361} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493976 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064736 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a73230d7-2608-5fda-9bd9-d380d1b68a15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062474} {:answer/id #uuid "1b5c7bcc-d988-51a2-9faf-29f8684be33d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062475} {:answer/id #uuid "51aa5b57-b075-512f-8ab1-4f61456e288d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062476} {:answer/id #uuid "54e07f76-634a-50b0-88d5-295949f8771b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062477} {:answer/id #uuid "62156e2a-34ce-55e9-88d2-edcd5c90f59d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062478}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186062477} :answer-field/id #uuid "13a25c94-9e58-52b2-9281-07182838de44" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062473}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "aa8dfac6-2e07-5a12-91bc-c7ca479ccc66" :question/math-academy-id "q-31382" :question/problem "What is the coefficient of ${x}^{2}y$ in the product $(x-y)({x}^{2}+6xy-9{y}^{2})?$" :question/worked-solution "We distribute all three terms of the binomial in the first set of parentheses to the trinomial in the second set of parentheses. Then, we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-y)({x}^{2}+6xy-9{y}^{2}) & = \\\\ x({x}^{2}+6xy-9{y}^{2})+(-y)({x}^{2}+6xy-9{y}^{2}) & = \\\\ {x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}y-9x{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}y-6x{y}^{2}+9{y}^{3} & = \\\\ {x}^{3}+5{x}^{2}y-15x{y}^{2}+9{y}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coefficient of ${x}^{2}y$ is $5.$"} :step/id #uuid "01cd63b9-1bc2-5c63-877d-2c84f5a29f2f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494043}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494038 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493900 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6186a0e8-15ad-5f04-9514-03a897bd46d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493536} {:answer/id #uuid "79daec01-1bd7-52c2-9729-762b7fa0a1f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493575} {:answer/id #uuid "7bcf2e2e-64a8-5b8a-80c5-fd2e2740d5d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493579} {:answer/id #uuid "89b4a778-8744-54f1-9b64-0291ebefd5aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493606} {:answer/id #uuid "db0402ea-ca02-5133-8b2b-0f9106ee55f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493735}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493536} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb22af84-6e18-5551-a3f1-bbcb3eb8cfb6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493889}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "222f2e72-f895-5b79-8f06-000077812dd5" :question/math-academy-id "q-78171" :question/problem "Given that  $({t}^{2}+t+2)({t}^{2}+2t+1)={t}^{4}+a{t}^{3}+b{t}^{2}+ct+2,$ calculate $a+b+c.$" :question/worked-solution "We distribute all three terms of the trinomial in the first set of parentheses to the trinomial in the second set of parentheses. Then, we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({t}^{2}+t+2)({t}^{2}+2t+1) & = \\\\ {t}^{2}({t}^{2}+2t+1)+t({t}^{2}+2t+1)+2({t}^{2}+2t+1) & = \\\\ {t}^{4}+2{t}^{3}+{t}^{2}+{t}^{3}+2{t}^{2}+t+2{t}^{2}+4t+2 & = \\\\ {t}^{4}+3{t}^{3}+5{t}^{2}+5t+2 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=3,$ $b=5,$ and $c=5.$ So, \n\n$$\na+b+c=3+5+5=13.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "97cc7e1e-8c02-52bb-8813-52ea962add8a"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494043 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493963 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1509f49e-da21-5888-a3e2-156f6abd8874" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493429} {:answer/id #uuid "39d6163e-8a28-5dea-acdf-43f43b916822" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493487} {:answer/id #uuid "45f89047-5bab-5a07-8abc-c0436c6ee8a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493509} {:answer/id #uuid "e27c97bd-9bdc-5a82-9295-1903f7e28d23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493750} {:answer/id #uuid "e8b629bc-bc84-51ab-bcdf-5cf5e9435232" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493763}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493750} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4c94381-cca8-57b8-bdf5-3cd85a1f90d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493877}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d9aae8a4-653e-5752-9c3b-a882c899846f" :question/math-academy-id "q-78170" :question/problem "Given that  $({p}^{2}+p+1)({p}^{2}+3p+1)={p}^{4}+a{p}^{3}+b{p}^{2}+cp+1,$ calculate $a+b+c.$" :question/worked-solution "We distribute all three terms of the trinomial in the first set of parentheses to the trinomial in the second set of parentheses. Then, we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({p}^{2}+p+1)({p}^{2}+3p+1) & = \\\\ {p}^{2}({p}^{2}+3p+1)+p({p}^{2}+3p+1)+1({p}^{2}+3p+1) & = \\\\ {p}^{4}+3{p}^{3}+{p}^{2}+{p}^{3}+3{p}^{2}+p+{p}^{2}+3p+1 & = \\\\ {p}^{4}+4{p}^{3}+5{p}^{2}+4p+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=4,$ $b=5,$ and $c=4.$ So, \n\n$$\na+b+c=4+5+4=13.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "9f30599b-eb03-56eb-8b41-df7c06969977" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494038}}] :activity/title "Multiplying Polynomials" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493378}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 720.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088543} :activity/id #uuid "cfe67386-85ee-52bc-a19f-9e5f91df386d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049840 :topic/id #uuid "17f87fcb-3fe5-5ecd-ba54-8883ffee898f" :topic/math-academy-id 839} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088543 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088991 :tutorial/content "The **standard form** equation of a line is \n\n$$\nax+by=c,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $a,b,$ and $c$ are all integers,\n\n- $a \\geq 0,$ and\n\n- $a,b,$ and $c$ have no common factors.\n\nNote that we always write $x$ *before* $y$ on the left-hand side!\n\nLet's see some examples:\n\n- The equation $2x - y= -5$ is in standard form. Indeed, the variables are on the left-hand side, while the constant term is on the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{2x - y}_{\\textrm{variables}} \\quad = \\underbrace{-5\\phantom{y}}_{\\textrm{constant}}\n$$\n\n \nAll coefficients are integers (namely, $2,$ $-1,$ and $-5$) and don't have common factors greater than $1.$ Also, the coefficient of $x$ is non-negative ($2$).\n\n- The equation $-3x + y = 1$ is *not* in standard form because the coefficient of $x$ is negative ($-3$).\n\n- The equation $2x+4y = -10$ is *not* in standard form because the coefficients $(2,4,$ and $-10)$ have a common factor of $2.$\n\n- The equation $\\dfrac12x+\\dfrac13y = 6$ is *not* in standard form because the coefficients of $x$ and $y$ are not integers." :tutorial/id #uuid "739ff387-5940-547f-adac-8b2ac8e5a229" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9126 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "0f6a809a-5a39-577f-8881-246e8ed35867" :step/math-academy-id 178720 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088544}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088544 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087709 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087437 :question/id #uuid "5faa755c-1e2d-52a7-a521-49f79b94ec39" :question/math-academy-id "e-14709"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e2458e5-8061-5f3f-9b4f-4d0bdd0ec641" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049935} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050581}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088071} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088074} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088219} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493927} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493955}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equations Written in Standard Form"} :step/id #uuid "e1825121-20e1-5854-9a35-360f43ffb277" :step/math-academy-id 178721 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088545}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088545 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088993 :tutorial/content "Let's now discuss how to convert the equation of a line to standard form.\n\nTo illustrate, we will take the line $y = 2x-1$ and convert it into standard form. We begin by moving all of the variables to the left-hand side and all of the constants to the right-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2x-1 \\\\ y-2x & =-1 \\\\ -2x+y & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow the equation is in the form $ax+by=c.$ However, it violates the rule $a \\geq 0.$ We can fix this by multiplying the equation by $-1$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x+y & =-1 \\\\ (-1)\\cdot (-2x+y) & =(-1)\\cdot (-1) \\\\ 2x-y & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $a,b,$ and $c$ are integers with no common factors, and $a \\geq 0.$ So, we are done!\n\nThe standard form of the line is $2x-y=1.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "914fb469-510e-5e74-92bc-6174870cbd47" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9221 :tutorial/title "Converting a Line to Standard Form"} :step/id #uuid "1ad80f23-5479-5329-8fd1-39cf7d32c8f0" :step/math-academy-id 178725 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088546}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088546 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087710 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087438 :question/id #uuid "7a4196bc-1f72-5435-9c00-c1fbef60b549" :question/math-academy-id "e-14710"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f399f6ed-91bc-594e-9a40-b7c79f60c5dc" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050622}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088065} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088299} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088300} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235494} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493934} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493940} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523946} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551270} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575567}] :knowledge-point/title "Converting from Slope-Intercept Form to Standard Form"} :step/id #uuid "219a19a2-5ca7-55ec-b664-706e0003f956" :step/math-academy-id 178726 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088547}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088547 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087711 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087439 :question/id #uuid "9e0c4000-c24e-5761-8cbe-60699b557eca" :question/math-academy-id "e-14711"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "daa37544-c4ec-5b8b-91a5-ccf5ee8f50d6" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088002} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088069} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493922} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493928} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501171} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526035}] :knowledge-point/title "Converting an Arbitrary Line to Standard Form"} :step/id #uuid "54390c19-603c-54bd-bf54-163d31414029" :step/math-academy-id 178730 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088548}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088548 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087712 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087440 :question/id #uuid "f78832f7-acd3-5779-b171-0650782b918e" :question/math-academy-id "e-14712"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eefba93e-12c7-5835-80fb-b36463f140d8" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049061} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050635}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088066} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088070} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088301} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493891} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501182} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501213}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Standard Equation of a Line Passing Through Two Points"} :step/id #uuid "5756f274-95fd-5077-9cb0-2b24aadadb72" :step/math-academy-id 178733}] :activity/title "Equations of Lines in Standard Form" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087911}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046648} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501288} :activity/id #uuid "ea710b72-1ae9-594f-81f0-bb4e388075ac" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049840 :topic/id #uuid "17f87fcb-3fe5-5ecd-ba54-8883ffee898f" :topic/math-academy-id 839} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501256 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501171 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ff46960-a634-5a81-ae1f-2ca39d897a7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3x=4y-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500593} {:answer/id #uuid "79487712-6da4-5496-8225-aac3af672e2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4y=3x+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500719} {:answer/id #uuid "b8f9749e-7d8c-55a2-9037-1039c6ab8a8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-2y=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500852} {:answer/id #uuid "dd48f16b-2a4a-5029-a026-63bc2965823a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3x-4y+3=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500938} {:answer/id #uuid "ec6efbc0-8d44-5de2-87c8-4041efd1a2f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3x-4y=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500972}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500972} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ecaf1bd-48c2-52f6-ac2e-e2c732914f1d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501059}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "654a7d79-4ae5-544e-b9a0-d1ca56a9cbc1" :question/math-academy-id "q-109618" :question/problem "Write $3-4y=-3x$ in standard form." :question/worked-solution "Our goal is to write the equation in the form $ax+by=c,$ where $a,b,$ and $c$ are integers with no common factors and $a\\ge 0.$\n\nWe begin by moving all of the variables to the left-hand side and all of the constants to the right-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n3x-4y=-3.\n$$\n\nNow, $a,b,$ and $c$ are integers with no common factors, and $a\\ge 0.$ So we are done!\n\nThe standard form of the line is $3x-4y=-3.$"} :step/id #uuid "20f07fd1-d467-5612-a99b-42e2c6facda8"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501288 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501182 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4948554b-8702-53e2-9b75-d60c9757c1b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3x-2y=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500613} {:answer/id #uuid "81c06851-5a55-515e-b014-4e1a61503690" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2y=3x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500734} {:answer/id #uuid "9a1bd908-d894-5634-a386-eae6d2558b62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7x-4y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500780} {:answer/id #uuid "ace9b919-181f-5f85-aacf-3f2308c62bcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-\\frac{4}{7}\\,y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500824} {:answer/id #uuid "fc01bcb9-ef79-5851-a139-46cdb6164d5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4y=7x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501004}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500780} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf0b3dce-d893-5070-8809-d9f2b47f02b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501112}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "7638fe82-2585-52d0-beb7-40611addaef6" :question/math-academy-id "q-44202" :question/problem "What is the equation of the line that passes through the points $(0,0)$ and $(4,7)$, expressed in standard form?" :question/worked-solution "First, we will construct the point-slope form of the line. Then, we will simplify it into standard form.\n\nTo construct the point-slope form of the line, we need a point and a slope. We already have two points, so we calculate the slope $m$ using the given points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}=\\frac{7-0}{4-0}=\\frac{7}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use this slope along with one of the points to write the point-slope form equation of the line. It doesn't matter which point we choose, but let's choose $(0,0).$ The point-slope equation is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-0 & =\\frac{7}{4}(x-0) \\\\ y & =\\frac{7}{4}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo simplify the point-slope formula into standard form, we first multiply by $4$ to get rid of the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{7}{4}(x) \\\\ 4\\cdot (y) & =4\\cdot \\frac{7}{4}(x) \\\\ 4y & =7x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we move the variables to the left side and the constant to the right side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4y & =7x \\\\ -7x+4y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply by $-1$ to make the $x$-coefficient positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7x+4y & =0 \\\\ (-1)\\cdot (-7x+4y) & =(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 7x-4y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the standard form of the equation is $7x-4y=0.$"} :step/id #uuid "b7421da9-e8bc-5823-9858-330f84d2ecf1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501292}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501292 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501213 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa1d2cc4-2934-5c01-acf3-e4688de64dff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4x-y=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500819} {:answer/id #uuid "c0d0840e-b8f1-5833-899d-775ab6668a32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x-y=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500867} {:answer/id #uuid "c3d3f7b4-e921-553d-88ad-be60876e12b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=2x-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500877} {:answer/id #uuid "ca9d0db1-1d42-546b-8c61-bc0bc89d225c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4x+y=-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500896} {:answer/id #uuid "cf99202c-1f33-5c86-9aa3-ff042e4be5ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+2y=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500913}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500819} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ee1d5f3-79b0-529f-9d27-584c54dd513d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501034}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b34b9e0f-ca1a-53ee-8948-5e07722771b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-72425" :question/problem "What is the equation of the line that passes through the points $(1,-2)$ and $(3,6)$, expressed in standard form?" :question/worked-solution "First, we will construct the point-slope form of the line. Then, we will simplify it into standard form.\n\nTo construct the point-slope form of the line, we need a point and a slope. We already have two points, so we calculate the slope $m$ using the given points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}=\\frac{6-(-2)}{3-1}=\\frac{8}{2}=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use this slope along with one of the points to write the point-slope form equation of the line. It doesn't matter which point we choose, but let's choose $(1,-2).$ The point-slope equation is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-2) & =4(x-1) \\\\ y+2 & =4(x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we simplify the point-slope formula into standard form, moving the variables to the left side and the constant to the right side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+2 & =4(x-1) \\\\ y+2 & =4x-4 \\\\ y & =4x-6 \\\\ -4x+y & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply by $-1$ to make the $x$-coefficient positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4x+y & =-6 \\\\ (-1)\\cdot (-4x+y) & =(-1)\\cdot (-6) \\\\ 4x-y & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the standard form of the equation is $4x-y=6.$"} :step/id #uuid "dbbb55d2-6ac7-5cb1-a4a3-8f1b82a1b491" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501256}}] :activity/title "Equations of Lines in Standard Form" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500468}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526118} :activity/id #uuid "5fd46b7c-ef09-527e-bc1f-fb6f5543dee9" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049840 :topic/id #uuid "17f87fcb-3fe5-5ecd-ba54-8883ffee898f" :topic/math-academy-id 839} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526118 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088300 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ce635f8-4383-5e2d-baa8-462f0646a57d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=2x-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525359} {:answer/id #uuid "56dcaada-2e45-5e47-b10d-7423de786283" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y-2x=10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525431} {:answer/id #uuid "a64fc9d9-1954-54bb-9104-2c70061145e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x+y=10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525626} {:answer/id #uuid "a9fc8576-442e-5448-9c72-26dd2ff8c409" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x-y=10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525635} {:answer/id #uuid "b0e5661e-64af-5f76-ad7e-bb04261a05bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x+y-10=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525654}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525635} :answer-field/id #uuid "8bedc031-1af4-59be-8827-ddccc40055ed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086454}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1ddc1b00-1388-5ab1-b4ec-fe79f4a369ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-7375" :question/problem "Write $y=2x-10$ in standard form." :question/worked-solution "Our goal is to write the equation in the form $ax+by=c,$ where $a,b,$ and $c$ are integers with no common factors and $a\\ge 0.$\n\nWe begin by moving all of the variables to the left-hand side and all of the constants to the right-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n-2x+y=-10\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply both sides of the equation by $-1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x+y & =-10 \\\\ (-1)\\cdot (-2x+y) & =(-1)\\cdot (-10) \\\\ 2x-y & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $a,b,$ and $c$ are integers with no common factors, and $a\\ge 0.$ So we are done!\n\nThe standard form of the line is $2x-y=10.$"} :step/id #uuid "5531d2d2-4653-5821-ad34-95da930170ae" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526148}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526128 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088065 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50e5beee-cd5d-5d0e-a06c-ed2bcb868f66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x+10y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525416}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525416} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca145851-e9d5-53d0-b524-37f7de7eeb0b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085124} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7c83729c-0f7e-5ffe-bf01-d09e27351dab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525515}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525515} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f29fa38-38be-5d7a-8b04-017c959f4e64" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085125}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "b90758e4-7aff-5ee7-bbb0-64e72c86781c" :question/math-academy-id "q-208783" :question/problem "Express the equation of the line $y=-\\frac{1}{5}x+\\frac{1}{2}$ in standard form.\n\n{{field-1}}\n\n$=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Our goal is to write the equation in the form $ax+by=c,$ where $a,b,$ and $c$ are integers with no common factors and $a\\ge 0.$\n\nWe begin by moving all of the variables to the left-hand side and all of the constants to the right-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{5}x+y=\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply both sides of the equation by $10:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5}x+y & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 10\\cdot (\\frac{1}{5}x+y) & =10\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ 2x+10y & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $a,b,$ and $c$ are integers with no common factors, and $a\\ge 0.$ So we are done!\n\nThe standard form of the line is \n\n$$\n2x+10y=5.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "81bb2396-2021-540b-9bce-0c126fd371de"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526148 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526035 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01ceb895-6483-595f-b812-ce7792346c53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+10y=-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525213} {:answer/id #uuid "38916548-028a-5017-811a-5ecb8cc9b06a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+11y=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525345} {:answer/id #uuid "669b434e-e144-50c0-890a-a5e64976a180" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-11y=-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525468} {:answer/id #uuid "9f12e004-fb3b-598c-9d12-4da7ab0cd767" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x+11y=-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525612} {:answer/id #uuid "f2361365-100c-59dd-ae92-0d3dd51e9ba8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+11y=-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525811}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525811} :answer-field/id #uuid "38e9167e-6f24-5db2-a3c7-7d0d60e4c2f7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525876}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "704b5a22-e7b8-5a05-8c48-94b98e94c6e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-22753" :question/problem "Write $2x+22y=-8$ in standard form." :question/worked-solution "Our goal is to write the equation in the form $ax+by=c,$ where $a,b,$ and $c$ are integers with no common factors and $a\\ge 0.$\n\nThe equation is in the form $ax+by=c.$ However, $a,b,$ and $c$ have a common factor of $2.$ So, we divide by $2$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+22y & =-8 \\\\ \\frac{2x+22y}{2} & =\\frac{-8}{2} \\\\ x+11y & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $a,b,$ and $c$ are integers with no common factors, and $a\\ge 0.$ So we are done!\n\nThe standard form of the line is $x+11y=-4.$"} :step/id #uuid "e46359de-50c7-51f6-b170-e204f33ff01e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526128}}] :activity/title "Equations of Lines in Standard Form" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525201}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 720.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116243} :activity/id #uuid "ab69be82-a737-5ca5-bd57-46d2a37c64bb" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050680 :topic/id #uuid "5e676adf-aa45-5e67-b4e5-57ec40661f6e" :topic/math-academy-id 1576} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116243 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116417 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to find all of the values of $x$ that satisfy *either* $4x+2 < 6$ *or* $2x \\geq 8.$\n\nTo do this, we solve each inequality individually and then compute the union of the two solutions.\n\nWe start by solving $4x+2 < 6\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+2 & <6 \\\\ 4x & <4 \\\\ x & <1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/0d/0d36b8e68d9fd0b33b255502860481eeb525b60382029b17434bfb072cc39da2.png)\n\nThen, we solve $2x \\geq 8\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & \\ge 8 \\\\ x & \\ge 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/ed/edf5665b7cab7f51e3f252601a96d43c6f0cbfc64e5c9b8da81f0ef09d7b2f5a.png)\n\nTo graph the values of $x$ that satisfy either inequality, we overlay the two graphs on the same number line:\n\n![](images/5c/5c60839a626f2ba8a0fe4a24f4011af0fa52c00d79fbf80039bcfdbdf0a612c7.png)\n\nThus, the final solution is $x < 1$ or $x \\geq 4.$ We can write this using interval notation as\n\n$$\n(-\\infty, 1) \\cup [4,\\infty).\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "6d186c13-c1df-52d0-8ed6-a383185138a9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1428 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "cdc53761-47a4-5dc1-a8a0-b21a01b5b35a" :step/math-academy-id 3201 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116245}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116244 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115321 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115153 :question/id #uuid "abae2e99-53b7-5d81-860c-9555ce50ec3a" :question/math-academy-id "e-965"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cdaed02f-09f2-5572-a70c-0e6979ef2167" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115903} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115905} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510386} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557245} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557318} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573939}] :knowl